<h2 id="formatted-writing">Bring formatted writing from a document</h2>
<ol><li>Copy an article selection from Word, Google Docs, or a web page.</li><li>In the workspace or a converter, select <strong>Import formatted writing</strong>. Paste into the empty import box. If it already contains pending text, select all before replacing it.</li><li>Choose <strong>Preview selected Word file or pasted article</strong>. Inspect both the reading preview and the converted Markdown, including the listed omissions.</li><li>For a table without marked header cells, check <strong>Use the first table row as headers</strong> only if that row contains column names, then preview again.</li><li>Acknowledge that you reviewed the conversion, then choose <strong>Import into editor</strong>. Confirm replacement if you have an article already. Source notes are kept, previous valid writing is checkpointed, and export approval is cleared.</li></ol>
<p>Try <strong>Preview a formatted example</strong> in the import panel to see the flow with fictional writing. It stages an example for review without applying it. Normal pasting in the article editor still inserts plain text or Markdown.</p>
<h3>HTML files and clipboard differences</h3><p>A selected .html or .htm file is detected as HTML in automatic mode. You can also choose Formatted selection or HTML and paste HTML source. Files must be UTF-8 and at most 512 KiB. Raw HTML pasted in automatic Markdown mode remains literal Markdown; choose HTML deliberately to convert it. You can also choose a Word .docx file directly, as described below. Some source applications or clipboard permissions supply only plain text, which cannot recover lost document structure.</p>
<h3>What the conversion supports</h3><p>Common HTML headings, paragraphs, line breaks, bold and italic text, safe absolute web links, code, single-line nested lists, and simple tables with a header row. Common bold and italic span styles are recognized, but arbitrary document CSS is not applied. An opening H1 becomes the title field. The converted article supports up to 60,000 characters; escaping literal punctuation can increase its size. Lists support six levels and 200 items; tables support twelve columns and 100 data rows.</p>
<h3>Review changes before applying</h3><p>Fonts, colors, page layout, and unsupported inline formatting are not preserved. Images become text placeholders with any supplied alternative description. Embedded and interactive content and explicitly hidden content are omitted. Unsafe or relative link targets are removed while labels remain. Nested emphasis and paragraph breaks within list items or cells may be simplified, with notices. Merged or nested tables, tracked changes, reversed or lettered lists, unusual nesting, and overly complex or oversized HTML are rejected before replacing your article. Resolve those structures in your source or import a smaller selection.</p>
<p>HTML is parsed locally in detached template contents. Original source nodes are never attached to the page. The reading preview is built from converted Markdown, not the original HTML. No source images or embedded resources are loaded, and no upload or AI request is made. Original HTML, choices, and pending previews are temporary and excluded from backups. After applying, only the converted article fields become workspace writing. This is article preparation, not a round-trip document editor; inspect your destination before publishing.</p>

<h3>Open a Word .docx directly</h3><p>For a file-first starting point, use the <a href="/word-to-wordpress/">Word to WordPress converter</a>. It uses the same local preview, checkpoint protection, and exports. Save current writing before changing tools.</p><ol><li>Select <strong>Import formatted writing</strong>, then <strong>Choose a Word document or article file</strong>. Select a plain .docx up to 8 MiB. No file is uploaded.</li><li>Inspect the automatically staged reading preview, Markdown, page details, and notices. Ordinary paragraphs, heading styles, bold and italic runs, safe web links, bullets, consecutive decimal lists, and simple header tables are supported.</li><li>If a table has no marked header, choose the first-row header option only when its first row contains column names, then preview the selected file again.</li><li>Acknowledge the conversion, then import. Cancelling replacement keeps your current writing and approval. Applying checkpoints valid previous writing, keeps source notes, replaces article fields and review notes, and clears approval.</li></ol><p>Only the main document is converted. Images and embedded objects become visible text placeholders. Headers, footers, comments, document metadata, fonts, colors, and page layout are not imported. Explicitly hidden text is omitted. Keep your original file and compare the conversion with it. Tracked changes, fields (including automatic tables of contents), footnotes/endnotes, equations, merged or nested tables, lettered/custom numbering, and unsupported structures are rejected. Resolve or simplify those in Word first, or import a smaller formatted selection. Old .doc, macro-enabled, encrypted, ZIP64, damaged, and unusually packaged files are not supported.</p><p>Word files use a bounded local ZIP reader: at most 256 entries, 32 MiB of declared expanded content, 2 MiB per selected XML part, and 4 MiB of selected XML in total. Only six known XML parts can be decompressed, with size and checksum checks; images and other file bytes are never extracted. XML is parsed in detached documents, then converted to the same bounded Markdown format used by the HTML importer. Modern browser support for raw DEFLATE decompression is required for compressed files; if unavailable, use a formatted selection. The converted article still has a 60,000-character limit. Selected bytes, previews, and import choices are temporary, excluded from backups, and cleared on apply or clearing the import.</p>
